# Formatting

## Switch cases

Indent `case` and `default` one level under `switch`. Indent the body of each case one level under the label.

```swift
switch viewModel.state {
    case .idle, .loading:
        ProgressView()
    case .empty:
        EmptyStateView()
    case .loaded(let movies, let activity):
        MovieList(movies: movies, activity: activity)
    case .failed(let error):
        ErrorStateView(error: error) { await viewModel.load() }
}
```

A single-line case uses the same label indent:

```swift
switch kind {
    case .movie: "film"
    case .tv: "tv"
    case .person: "person.fill"
}
```

Enum declarations keep `case` at the usual indent of the type body. This rule is for `switch` statements.

## Multiline arguments

When a call or initializer does not fit on one line, put the opening `(` on the first line and **indent every argument (and nested multiline call) one level under that line**. The closing `)` aligns with the start of the call.

```swift
// ✅ GOOD — continuation lines are indented under the call
ListMembershipButton(
    draft: content.detail.listItem(),
    lists: lists,
    index: listsIndex
) {
    viewModel.noteListSaveFailed()
}

// ❌ BAD — arguments line up with the callee instead of indenting
MovieDetailRouteView(
movieID: id,
movies: movies,
router: router
)
```

Single-line calls stay on one line. This applies to initializers, function calls, and SwiftUI builders alike.

## File layout

Prefer names that show up in the Project navigator. Split when a type is its own thing; keep composition helpers in the screen file.

### Keep in the screen file

- **`@ViewBuilder` properties and methods** on the screen (`searchBody`, `shelfGrid`, `heroSection`) that only organize the same screen’s `body`.
- **Small private helpers** tied to one layout decision (a modifier chain, a one-off stack).
- **Implementation-only types** (`CodingKeys`, paging scratch structs) that are not UI.

### Extract to their own file

- **Named `View` types** another developer would search for (`AwardShelfCard`, `CreditsListRow`, `ListChangeBanner`).
- **Reusable rows, sections, and sheets** used from more than one call site in the feature, or large enough to read on their own.
- **Route wrappers** and tab roots when they carry real layout (`MovieDetailRouteView`, `BrowseTabRoot`).
- **Screen payload types and feature enums** when the view model file is large or tests need the type alone (`MovieDetailContent`, `SearchScope`).

Rough guides: consider splitting a **view file past ~250 lines** or a **view model past ~350** when the extra type would get a clear filename. Do not split only to hit a line count.

See also [`swiftui.mdc`](.cursor/rules/swiftui.mdc) for when to extract subviews from an oversized `body`.
